The code statements that lead to the bug are:
<text>sysfs_emit(buf, "%s\n", goal->path ? goal->path : "");</text>
<text>kfree(goal->path);
goal->path = path;</text>
Reason:
The `path_show()` function reads `goal->path` without holding `damon_sysfs_lock`, while `path_store()` frees and replaces `goal->path` without any lock. This allows a concurrent write (via a separate file descriptor) to free the buffer that `path_show()` is reading, causing a use-after-free.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
<text>if (!mutex_trylock(&damon_sysfs_lock))
    return -EBUSY;
len = sysfs_emit(buf, "%s\n", goal->path ? goal->path : "");
mutex_unlock(&damon_sysfs_lock);</text>
<text>if (!mutex_trylock(&damon_sysfs_lock)) {
    kfree(path);
    return -EBUSY;
}
kfree(goal->path);
goal->path = path;
mutex_unlock(&damon_sysfs_lock);</text>
Reason:
Both `path_show()` and `path_store()` now acquire `damon_sysfs_lock` before accessing or modifying `goal->path`. This ensures mutual exclusion, preventing the read from accessing freed memory. The `mutex_trylock()` returns `-EBUSY` if the lock is contended, which avoids blocking but still prevents the race.

